2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.bt.com.bn/golden-legacy/2011/06/13/antonio-pigafetta-bruneis-16th-century-chronicler
2 CONT
2 CONT Rozan Yunos BANDAR SERI BEGAWAN
2 CONT
2 CONT Monday, June 13, 2011 
2 CONT
2 CONT ONE of the most vivid descriptions about Brunei in the 16th century was provided by a European voyager named Pigafetta.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pigafetta was an Italian but was part of a Spanish crew exploring the Indies. On the return voyage to Spain, they travelled via the Pacific and Indian Oceans. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was during the return trip to Pigafetta stopped in Brunei and described Brunei very clearly. It was his description that many historians quoted quite extensively. His description is very comprehensive and covered many areas inc
2 CONC luding the first description the city entirely built in salt water.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parts of his description of Brunei include the following description of Kampong Ayer:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The city is built in the sea, the King's palace and the houses of the principal persons excepted. It contains twenty-five thousand hearths or families. The houses are built of wood upon large piles, to keep them from the water. W
2 CONC hen the tide rises, the women, who are chief venders of necessaries, traverse the town in boats. In front of the King's place is a large wall, built with bricks of great size, with embrazures, or rather port-holes, as in a fortres
2 CONC s; and on the wall are mounted fifty-six bombards of brass, and fix of iron: in course of two days we passed in the city, they made several discharges from these guns."
2 CONT
2 CONT Pigafetta described the Sultan as: "the King, who is a Mahometan, is called Rajah Siripada. He is very corpulent and may be about forty years of age. He is waited upon by women alone, the daughters of the chief inhabitants of the 
2 CONC island. No one is allowed to address him otherwise than in the manner I have described, through a sarbacane. He has ten secretaries constantly employed on different matters of state, who write on a very thin epidermis of certain t
2 CONC rees which is called chiritoles. He never leaves his palace upon any occasion other than to hunt."
2 CONT
2 CONT Who was Pigafetta? Pigafetta was named Cavallero Antonio Pigafetta born in 1491 and died in 1534. He was an Italian scholar and traveller from today's Austrian city of Venice then known as the Republic of Venice. In his book, he d
2 CONC escribed himself as "a gentleman of Vicenza, and Knight of Rhodes".
2 CONT
2 CONT Pigafetta himself came from a rich family and is independently wealthy. As a young man, he studied subjects related to seafaring and travel such as astronomy, cartography and geography. The voyage with Magellan was not his first. 
2 CONC He had served on the ships of the Knights of Rhodes at the beginning of the sixteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1519, Pigafetta accompanied the papal nuncio, Monsignor Chieregati, to Spain. It was in Seville, Spain that Antonio Pigafetta heard of Magellan's planned expedition and he decided to join the crew.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a translation from an 1819 book entitled "A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels in Various Parts of America", many of which are now first translated into English by John Pinkerton, Pigafetta 
2 CONC described,
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the year 1519, I was in Spain at the court of Charles V, King of the Romans, in company with Signor Chiericato, then apostolical prothonotary and orator of Pope Leo X of holy memory, who by his merits was raised to the dignity
2 CONC  of Bishop and Prince of Teramo."
2 CONT
2 CONT "An opportunity soon presented itself. I learnt that a squadron of five vessels was under equipment at Sevilla, destined for the discovery of the Molucca Islands, whence we derive our spices; and that Fernandez Magellan, a Portugu
2 CONC ese gentleman, and commander of the order of St Jago de la Spata, who had already more than once traversed the ocean with great reputation, was nominated captain-general of the expedition."
2 CONT
2 CONT Pigafetta accepted the title of sobrasaliente or supernumerary (temporary staff) and a pay of 1,000 maravedis. It was during the expedition Pigafetta became Magellan's assistant. He kept an accurate and meticulous journal througho
2 CONC ut the journey. There were 240 men who set out from Seville together with Pigafetta. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They travelled around the world exploring parts of the Indies, the Americas and later arrived in Asia. It was in Mactan, Philippines, that Magellan was killed by the natives there. Pigafetta himself was wounded. He recorded that M
2 CONC agellan was killed on April 27, 1521, and described the battle between the islanders and the Spanish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Juan Sebastian Elcano took over the leadership on the death of Magella. Pigafetta managed to recover. Captain Elcano together with Pigafetta was among 18 men out of the original 240 who limped back to Spain arriving at the
2 CONC  port in Salucar de Barrameda, Province of Cadiz, in September 1522.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pigafetta later returned to Venice. He wrote about his experiences in his book entitled "Relazione del primo viaggio intorno al mondo" ("Report on the First Voyage Around the World") originally published in Italian. The book was p
2 CONC ublished in French when Charles Amoretti translated it into French. The manuscript was not published in its entirety until the late eighteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pigafetta described that he wrote the book because "on my return to Italy, His Holiness the Sovereign Pontiff Clement VII, of whom I had the honour of an audience at Monterosi, and to whom I related the adventures of my voyage, re
2 CONC ceived me with great kindness, and told me that I should much oblige him in affording a copy of the journal of my voyage". Pigafetta dedicated his book and the Island of Rhodes to the Grand Master of Rhodes. The Brunei Times
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sultan Bolkiah (Nakhoda Ragam):  Who are the descendants of Sultan Bolkiah? - By: Mohd Abdullah Abd Ghani
2 CONT
2 CONT The genealogy of Sultan Bolkiah descendants are bit confusing. He was been recorded to have several wife including those who lived in Sulu, Philippines. He also been recorded in Philippine's royal family genealogy, however those r
2 CONC ecords from Philippines are not match to records that Brunei have. What we have here (in my Geni.com website) are still theory which derived from several  record comparison. Using this record as historical fact (regarding on Sulta
2 CONC n Bolkiah's descendent) are within reader's responsibility. Here are some of the relevant records about Sultan Bolkiah genealogy which may help:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Text extracted from: http://www.royalark.net/Brunei/brunei3.htm'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1513 - 1533 H.H. Sri Paduka Baginda Sultan Bolkiah Shah Alam ibni al-Marhum Sultan Sulaiman, Sultan and Yang di-Pertuan of Brunei Dar us-Salam. b. ca. 1481, son of Paduka Sri Sultan Sulaiman ibni al-Marhum Sultan Sharif 'Ali Berka
2 CONC t, Sultan of Brunei Dar us-Salam#5, educ. Java. In his reign Brunei became a great Imperial power in the region and extended its influence over large parts of Borneo and the Philippines, including the sultanates of Sulu and Maguin
2 CONC danao. Crowned before 19th March 1513. m. (first) a daughter of Datu Gamban, of Seluang, in Sulu. m. (second) Putri Laila Menjanai (bur. at the Kiangi, above the Upas), daughter of Sultan Nasuran, and granddaughter of Paduka Maula
2 CONC na Maha Sri Sultan Sharif al-Hashim 'Abu Bakar bin Sharif Zainal-Abidn, Sultan of Sulu. He d. 1533 #6 (bur. Sultan Bolkiah Mausoleum, Kota Batu), having had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT •1) H.H. Paduka Sri Sultan 'Abdu'l Kahar Jalil ul-Alam ibni al-Marhum Sultan Bolkiah Shah Alam, Sultan and Yang di-Pertuan of Brunei Dar us-Salam - see below.
2 CONT
2 CONT •2) Y.A.M. Paduka Sri Pangiran Bendahara Sri Maharaja Permaisuara Pangiran Muhammad Tajud-din.
2 CONT
2 CONT •3) Y.A.M. Pangiran Sri Maharaja Laila Pangiran Buong Manis (uncle of Rijal in Spanish accounts 1578). He quarrelled with Raja Sakam, who banished him to Kamanis. He then intrigued with the Spanish to invade Brunei in 1578. He d. 
2 CONC from cholera, at Brunei Town, August 1578, having had issue:  •a) A daughter. m. at Brunei Town, 1575, a son of Y.A.M. Pangiran Sri Maharaja Ratna. •b) Aother daughter. m. at Brunei Town, 26th June 1578, Don Agostin de Legazpi (k.
2 CONC  by the Spanish for rebellion, 1589), nephew of Datu Lacondola, of Tondo, in the Philippines.
2 CONT
2 CONT •4) Pangiran …He had issue, a son:  •a) Y.A.M. Pangiran Maharaja di-Raja (cousin of Rijal in Spanish accounts 1578). Appointed as Governor of the capitol by the Spanish, pending the return of the Sultan 1578. He fled to Sarawak wi
2 CONC th his family, after the Spanish withdrew from Brunei.
2 CONT
2 CONT •1) A daughter. m. the Raja of Maynilad, in Luzon, the Philippines. She had issue, a son:  ◾a) Raja Muhammad Matanda, of Maynilad. m. at Brunei, ca. 29th July 1521, his first cousin, a granddaughter of Sultan Bolkiah Shah Alam. He
2 CONC  d.s.p.l.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' Text extracted from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolkiah'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan Bolkiah was the sixth Sultan of Brunei. He ascended the throne of Brunei upon the abdication of his father, Sultan Sulaiman, and ruled Brunei from 1485 to 1524. His reign marked the Golden Age of Brunei[1] and saw the Sulta
2 CONC nate become the superpower of the Malay archipelago. Bolkiah frequently travelled abroad to gain new ideas for the development of the country, as well as seeking suggestions from his various chiefs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan Bolkiah's victory over Sulu and Seludong (modern day Manila),[2] as well as his marriages to Laila Mecana, the daughter of Sulu Sultan Amir Ul-Ombra, widened Brunei's influence in the Philippines.
2 CONT
2 CONT This increased Brunei's wealth as well as extending Islamic teachings in the region, resulting in the influence and power of Brunei reaching its peak during this period. Bolkiah's rule reached essentially all of coastal Borneo,[1]
2 CONC  as far south as Banjarmasin,[3] and as far north as the island of Luzon, including Seludong (present-day Manila) in the Philippines.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bolkiah was married to Laila Mecanai the daughter of Sulu Sultan Amir Ul-Ombra and Datu Kemin.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death, sultan Bolkiah was succeeded by his son, Abdul Kahar.[1] He was buried in Kota Batu with his wife, Princess Leila Mechanai.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest historical record of the Sultans of Brunei is not clearly known due to the poor early documentation of Brunei history. In addition there has been an effort to Islamise the history, with the "official history" not matc
2 CONC hing up with verifiable foreign sources [4] The Batu Tarsilah - the genealogical record of the kings of Brunei - was not started until 1807. Therefore, much of the intepretation on history relied on earlier Chinese sources and leg
2 CONC ends.[5] It seems that the early Sultanate of Brunei was dependent on Chinese support,[5][6][7] and perhaps early Sultans were of Chinese origin.[5] Furthermore the earliest Sultans may have been practising the Hindu or Buddhist r
2 CONC eligions, with early names indicating this origin.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes and references
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Jump up to: a b c d Sidhu, Jatswan S. (2009). "Bolkiah, Sultan (r. 1485–1524)". Historical Dictionary of Brunei Darussalam (second ed.). Lanham, Maryland: Scarecrow Press. p. 37. ISBN 978-0-8108-7078-9. 2.Jump up ^ Although th
2 CONC is is the interpretation based upon the work of Antonio Pigafetta, other authorities suggest that Seludong may have referred to the Serudong River, which is in northeastern Borneo, and not to the island of Luzon at all. Saunders, 
2 CONC Graham (2002). History of Brunei (second ed.). New York: RoutledgeCurzon. p. 42. ISBN 978-0-7007-1698-2. 3.Jump up ^ Saunders 2002, p. 45 4.^ Jump up to: a b "Brunei". 4dw.net. Retrieved 18 January 2015. 5.^ Jump up to: a b c Elis
2 CONC seeff, Vadime (January 2000). "Chapter 8: A Brunei Sultan of the Early Fourteenth Century - A Study of an Arabic Gravestone". The Silk Roads: Highways of Culture and Commerce. Berghahn Books. pp. 145–157. ISBN 978-1-57181-222-3. R
2 CONC etrieved 26 December 2013. 6.Jump up ^ "Malay History: What's Missing in Malaysian History Books". Malaysianunplug.blogspot.co.uk. Retrieved 18 January 2015. 7.Jump up ^ "The golden history of Islam in Brunei". The Brunei Times. R
2 CONC etrieved 18 January 2015.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kingdom of Tondo and its relation to Sultan Bolkiah.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Tondo (also referred to as Tundo, Tundun, Tundok, Lusung) was a fortified kingdom which was located in the Manila Bay area, specifically north of the Pasig River, on Luzon island. It is one of the settlements mentioned by the Phil
2 CONC ippines' earliest historical record, the Laguna Copperplate Inscription.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally an Indianized kingdom in the 10th century, Tondo built upon and capitalized on being central to the long-existing ancient regional trading routes throughout the archipelago to include among others, initiating diplomatic
2 CONC  and commercial ties with China during the Ming Dynasty. Thus it became an established force in trade throughout Southeast Asia and East Asia. (See Luções). Tondo's regional prominence further culminated during the period of its a
2 CONC ssociated trade and alliance with Brunei's Sultan Bolkiah, when around 1500 its peak age as a thalassocratic force in the northern archipelago was realized. When the Spanish first arrived in Tondo in 1570 and defeated the local ru
2 CONC lers in the Manila Bay area in 1591, Tondo came under the administration of Manila (a Spanish fort built on the remains of Kota Seludong), ending its existence as an independent state. This subjugated Tondo continues to exist toda
2 CONC y as a district of the city of Manila.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alliance with the Sultanate of Brunei (1500)
2 CONT
2 CONT Tondo became so prosperous that around the year 1500, the Kingdom of Brunei under Sultan Bolkiah merged it by a royal marriage of Gat Lontok, who later became Rajah of Namayan, and Dayang Kaylangitan to establish a city with the M
2 CONC alay name of Selurong (later to become the city of Maynila)[12][13] on the opposite bank of Pasig River. The traditional rulers of Tondo, the Lakandula, retained their titles and property upon embracing Islam but the real politica
2 CONC l power transferred to the master trader House of Sulayman, the Rajahs of Manila.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notable monarchs of Tondo[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jayadeva Senapati (Admiral) 900? ?  Lakan Timamanukum  1150? ?  Alon Lakan Alon 1200? ?  Gambang Lakan Gambang 1390? 1417?  Suko Lakan Suko 1417? 1430?  Lontok Lakan Lontok 1430? 1450?  Kalangitan Dayang Kaylangitan, Queen of Nama
2 CONC yan and Tondo 1450? 1515?  Salalila Rajah Salalila or Rajah Sulayman I 1515? 1558?  Matanda Rajah Matanda or Rajah Sulayman II or Rajah Ache, King of Namayan 1558? 1571  Lakan Dula Banaw Lakandula, King of Tondo and Sabag 1558? 15
2 CONC 71  Sulayman Rajah Sulayman, King of Tondo 1571 1585  Magat Salamat 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sultan Bolkiah (Nakhoda Ragam) in other references:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the most interesting facts about Sultan Bolkiah (Nakhoda Ragam) that I found are come from the unexpected source, which is a book with tittle The Soils of The Philippines  written by Authors: Carating, Rodelio B., Galanta, 
2 CONC Raymundo G., and Bacatio, Clarita D. This book is look like an ordinary soils science book, however what surprising me most is the contents of royal family history inside it (which we won't expect it to be appear in soil science b
2 CONC ook). About 30% of this book contents are history of royal families and rulers of ancient Philippines.
2 CONT
2 CONT In page 31 to 32 in this book which published by Springer, mention about Sultan Bolkiah or Nakhoda Ragam is the same person as Gat Lontok or Rajah Lontok who married to Dayang Kalangitan Datu Gamban which by this marriage have dau
2 CONC ghter known as Dayang Panginoan ibni Sultan Bolkiah who then married to Gat Balagtas, ruler of the Kingdom of Namayan, with tittle Rajah Salalila who converted to Islam when married with Dayang Panginoan ibni Sultan Bolkiah and be
2 CONC came known as Rajah Suleyman 1 de Salila of the Kingdom of Maynila.
